---
.title = "How Rooms works",
.description = "Why a room is something you hold, what a click promises, how your browser is a full peer, and how the code is divided.",
.date = .date("2026-09-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

Rooms is one app with two homes. In a browser it runs as a full glowcap peer
in WebAssembly. On a server the same Rust code runs over sockets, files, and
a database. Neither gets its own idea of what joining, sending, recovering,
or failing means.

# A room is something you hold

The server is the first thing your session reaches. It introduces you to
rooms and to other members. When you join a room, the room hands you a seat,
and everything you do there goes through it: posting, leaving, showing that
you are typing. Nobody can speak as you, because nobody else holds your
seat.

Every line you post comes back with its own reference for editing or
deleting it. A private room opens from a link, and an invitation works
once. Admin and archive access are references too, so a host can hand out
one narrow thing without adding roles to every message on the wire.

[The server gives a person and a bot the same room. The room introduces them to each other, and from then on they talk directly, with the room out of the way.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-rooms-capabilities.svg'))

A direct conversation starts with an introduction between three parties.
The server hands you an inbox the other member made just for you, and using
it opens a connection between the two of you. The server made the
introduction and then steps aside. It never carries the conversation.
[The Rooms protocol]($link.page('rooms/protocol')) names every one of these
objects and the calls they answer.

# What a click promises

A click first becomes a note of what you asked for. Rooms then does the work,
on disk or over the network, and records either what happened or the
problem it hit. The screen shows that record, including work still going
and work that needs you.

[A click becomes a saved request, Rooms does the work, the result is confirmed, and the screen shows what happened. A refused or unconfirmed request stays beside the same note, in view.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-rooms-intent.svg'))

You meet this in everyday actions:

| Action | What you asked for | What you see afterwards |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Join a room | The server, the room, and the name you chose or left blank. | Your seat in the room under the name you got, or a refusal that keeps your choice and the suggestion. |
| Send a line | Your exact text and files, for one conversation. | The confirmed line, or your text back in the box, or a line marked unconfirmed that you will not be asked to resend blindly. |
| Change where history goes | The archive you picked and whether to keep recording. | The new setting in force, or an erased history, or a visible problem while the old setting stays in force. |
| Open a shared session | The link and the access you asked for. | The session on screen, or a plain message saying whether the trouble was reaching it or being let in. |

Two files make this hold across restarts. One records what you want and
what has been confirmed. The other records work that was started and still
has to be checked against the outside world. A process reads both before it
says it is ready, so it never claims a clean start while an earlier write,
registration, or announcement is still unknown.

# Why Rust

Rooms is a working chat application built from Rust on glowcap's C ABI, so
the ABI carries a real product and not only examples. Zig could have done
everything Rooms does directly. Writing it in Rust tests the C ABI and the
WebAssembly build the way an outside user would.

The [application boundary](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0147-rooms-rust-application-boundary.md)
keeps the application rules in a Rust crate built without the standard
library, so it cannot touch files, sockets, or clocks. The services ship as binaries with no runtime
to install.

The cost is bundle size, build time, and a copy of each typed command where the
page meets WebAssembly.

# How the code is divided

[The browser and the server both stand on one shared runtime. Under it, one crate holds the rules and one holds the glowcap clients, which stand on the glowcap protocol core. Only the two top crates and the platform crate touch a browser or an operating system.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-rooms-layers.svg'))

| Crate | What it holds |
| --- | --- |
| `rooms-application` | The rules, with no I/O: limits, requests, state changes, name allocation, everything that can go wrong, and the shape of saved files. |
| `rooms-glowcap` | The clients for each Rooms reference, and the checks on what arrives over the wire, on top of glowcap's C ABI. |
| `rooms-runtime` | The order work runs in, what gets accepted, recovery, cleanup, and turning saved state into what the screen shows. |
| `rooms-platform` | Tokio, clocks, randomness, files, listening sockets, TLS, and WebSocket connections. |
| `rooms-services` | The server programs: the room server, the session service, the archive, the web server, and the IRC bridge and IRC server. |
| `rooms-browser` | Browser storage, WebRTC, the JavaScript side, the WebAssembly entry points, and the screen. |

Checks in the build keep each crate's imports going one way. The shared crates
cannot import the platform, the services, or the browser. The browser cannot
import the server side. The services may use the platform, but they cannot
push server behavior back into the shared rules.

# Your browser is a peer

The page loads the same glowcap protocol core every other language binding
uses. JavaScript supplies the clock and the byte connections. The Rust
browser code owns your session, what is saved, and WebRTC. TypeScript draws
the screen and turns what you do into requests.

Your browser reaches the room server through the web server's byte relay. The
relay forwards bytes without parsing them and holds nothing of yours. It could
still read them, and on an iroh route it checks the room server's key on your
behalf, so you are trusting the web server that gave you the page. For a direct
message, WebRTC puts the two peers on one channel, and the server only passes
along the connection details.

# When something goes wrong

A known problem has a stable code, a subject, a plain sentence, and, where
there is one, a suggested next step. A problem Rooms did not expect arrives in
the same shape with a short diagnostic. If cleaning up after a problem also
fails, the second failure is kept beside the first.

Rust and TypeScript share that shape, so the screen tells a taken name from an
unreachable route, an expired invitation, a denied reference, a storage
failure, or an unconfirmed request by its code, not its wording. A code it has
never seen is shown as unknown, never mistaken for success.

# What is stored where

A session kept on a device, the session service, and the archive service
each keep their own things, and moving or sharing one never quietly moves
the others. The session service keeps your saved state, the record of
unfinished work, your identity, which servers to connect to, your links, and
which archive you picked. An archive keeps messages and whatever files it
accepted. A device attached to a shared session keeps only its display
settings and its local archive. A session kept locally is the device's own,
saved in the browser's storage.

So a shared session outlives every device it is opened on, while each device
keeps its own theme, open panels, and local history. Drafts and the selected
conversation belong to the shared session. Clearing a session does not erase
an archive somebody else still holds.

Continue with [Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations')) to host it, or
[Run Rooms]($link.page('rooms/start')) to see all of this on screen.
